Siggi Engelbrecht wrote:
> 
> After setting up a machine with DU 4.0E and using the graphical
> 'checkbox' for carrying out various tasks, sendmail put wrong
> reply adresses into outgoing mails.
> 
> This could be corrected for by using mailsetup. BUT, despite most
> reasonable configurations, e-mails leaving the top domain can NOT
> be replied to by the reply function or using the (as far as I can
> see) CORRECT address.
> 
> I have an alias address pointing to the very same machine. Could this
> cause the problem?
> 
> Taken together:
> 
> I am sitting at dec533au2.biologie.uni-osnabrueck.de.
> Using engel@dec533au2.biologie.uni-osnabrueck.de as mail address
> works inside the domain, but not from outside.
> Using the alias engel@uos.de (which points to
> engel@dec533au2.biologie.uni-osnabrueck.de) works from everywhere
> but there is, naturally, no way to get sendmail to put this alias
> into the mail header - at least not as far as I am aware of.
> 

dec533au2.biologie.uni-osnabrueck.de does not appear to be reachable
from the Internet on port 25 and it has no MX records defined that would
route mail addressed to it via an accessible host. If the user names on
dec533au2 are not identical to those at uos.de and resolve to the same
mailbox addresses, your only alternative is to correct the DNS entry for
your host to provide a path to it through your firewall by way of MX
records. For example:

	dec533au2	IN MX 1 dec533au2
	dec533au2	IN MX 10 deimos.rz.Uni-Osnabrueck.DE.

Only if the usernames on dec533au2 are identical to those on uos.de
(that is, name@dec533au2 resolves to the same user address as
name@uos.de) can you can "masquerade" all outgoing addresses as uos.de.
If the setup menus do not provide you with the options you want, edit
the resulting sendmail.cf file to put the desired domain in the macro
that defines the exported/masquerade domain. This should be defined on a
line beginning "DW". If you are using the Berkeley M4 macros to
construct your sendmail.cf, it will be on a line beginning "DM". In
either case, the line should be preceeded by a comment describing its
purpose. 

-Tom 
