Udo Grabowski <udo.grabowski@imk.fzk.de> writes:

>Everything is mounted on it's place, but when I'm 'grabow' and type
>a 'pwd' or do a filename inquiry in Fortran, my path appears as
>'/tmp_mnt/home/grabow' instead of '/home/grabow'. Is it possible
>to setup automount to use directly the specified mount points like
>a static mount would do ?

Not without implementing autofs, which we'd like to do someday.
/home is what I call an "intercept point", visible if you do
"mount -e".  It's an NFS mountpoint that points to a limited
NFS within automount.  When you reference /home/grabow, automount
catches the reference, mounts imksun:/export/home2/grabow on
/tmp_mnt/home/grabow, then says that /home/grabow is a symbolic
link to /tmp_mnt/home/grabow.

The biggest problems with the scheme are related to things like "at"
or cron trying to cd to /tmp_mnt/home/grabow long after it's been unmounted.
Some shells (bash and ksh come to mind) implement pwd as a builtin
and will report /home/grabow, at least they will if you cd there.
You may also be able to get /home/grabow into a Fortran program by
fetching the PWD environment variable.

	-Ric Werme

