Arne Vajhoj wrote (in reply to someone):
>> Does anyone know of a product that will dis-assemble VAX / VMS objects?
>> The original source program was written in Fortran many years ago.  It
>> would be extremely useful to be able to re-generate the source (or the
>> macro equivalent), so that we can maintain this system.
>
>DISM32 (available at FTP.WKU.EDU) will disassemble VMS VAX EXE-files
>(you can LINK/SHARE the OBJ's to create an EXE).
>
>DISM32 is quite a good program. But I will still consider the whole
>approach hope-less for larger applications. Macro-code with
>"automatic" names and no comments is not very usefull for 
>maintaining a program !

I did not see the original post of this, but concur with Arne's final
paragraph. 

If memory serves me there is another program called UNMACRO from the
same site, 
I cannot compare them as I have only tried DISM32.

In my experience, unless you have unlimited time on your hands
disassembling 
anything other than small executables for future enhancement/maintenance
is 
worse than useless.

Regardless of the original source language, a disassembler can only
generate the 
assembly code of the OS, in our case MACRO.  (There may be attempts "out
there" 
to re-create the source language from the assembly code, but I've not
heard of 
any, nor would feel inclined to trust a second level of disassembly.)

I would guess that the original poster is referring to a small to medium 
program, so there might be several strategies to get the desired source
for 
future maintenance.

The following comments are therefore offered only as "food for thought".
I have 
only been involved in "disassembly" for only a couple of very trivial
programs.

1). I would attempt to recreate the program from knowledge of the input
and 
output and any documentation, no matter how sparse, e.g. "XYZ's
algorithm was 
used".  Obviously any HLL can be used, but I would tend to use the
original 
language in that imitation of output might be easier - this is, however,
a moot 
point.  Bear in mind that in many (most) programs, no matter how
technical, the 
source code content will be about 75-80% I/O and housekeeping and the
remainder 
technical.  The former should be easy to recreate because you've got the
input 
and output in front of you.

2). If the above is successful for a typical case, try as many boundary 
conditions as possible on the original and your new executable.  Note
that you 
may have to actually introduce bugs that you will eventually fix in
order to get 
boundary cases equivalent.

3). If a "tricky" (i.e. difficult) algorithm is employed, try to isolate
that 
part of the code in the disassembled code and try to manually convert
that back 
to what Fortran would have translated to it. (You've got to use a lot of
GOTO's 
here :-).  At least, you are now working with a more manageable section
of the 
disassembled macro, and can better survive the ""automatic" names and no 
comments" that Arne mentioned.

4). Beyond this, I believe you will have to employ the services of a
specialist.

1)-3) were broadly the tactics that I used for the couple of trivial
cases that 
I have experienced, and luckily I achieved success.  There may be other
tactics 
which perhaps others might advise.

BTW Arne, what is the coding for your name?  I assume from different
ways I have 
seen it that it is an ellided o and e?  Pure curiosity, not VMS :-)

