Both of these declare array objects with one dimension, having four
elements.

>main(){ printf("sizes of foo and bar are %d, %d\n", sizeof(foo), sizeof(bar)); }
>
># cc  -g -O0 ct.c  -o ct
>cc: Error: ct.c, line 4: Missing "]".

Ah, you are being bitten by the fact that 3, 4 is not a constant expression.
Though this is a strange way to report the required diagnostic. :)

The expressions which specify array dimensions must be constant.  Expressions
that have a comma operator in them are not considered constant expressions,
even though both of the constituents may be constant expressions.

>int bar[3,4];
>
>          constant-expression comma-operator constant-expression
>
>   still a constant-expression or not?  If not, why not (and it

It's certainly an expression with a value that can be determined at compile
time, no argument there.  However, that is not the same as a constant
expression. The latter is just a term that is arbitrarily defined in the C
standard. You have to apply that definition to determine whether something
is a constnat expression or not.

>John
>PS, FWIW, gcc accepts both of these and produces the 'expected' results

It does not if you tell it to be an ANSI C compiler witn -ansi -pedantic.

I tried it with egcs 2.91.57, which accepts it without the -pedantic
option, but produces the following warning if you turn on -pedantic:

	warning: ANSI C forbids variable-size array 'a'.

You have fallen into a common pitfall by assuming that your compiler is a C
compiler by default. Many compilers default to supporting their implementor's
extensions, with ANSI C support being a negative option. For example, the
Microsoft compiler supports transparent unions and // comments by
default.  GCC supports // comments, statement blocks that behave like
expressions, array sizes that are computed at run time, nested functions and
other goodies.



