>}It does not if you tell it to be an ANSI C compiler witn -ansi -pedantic.
>
>   Actually I tried it with -ansi -Wall
>   but the man page scared me away from -pedantic :)

The -ansi option just means to disable GCC extensions that would cause ansi
programs to be rejected or diagnosed. The -pedantic option is needed to further
comply with ANSI C by generating required diagnostics for syntax and constraint
violations.

That (outdated) man page is just GNU propaganda: ``there is no reason to use
this option; it exists only to satisfy pedants''. This is, of course, nonsense.
The list of syntax and constraint violations required by ANSI C is not all that
extensive; to say that only a pedant would want these diagnostics is laughable.

If your compiler doesn't diagnose an ANSI C syntax or constraint violation, the
code may well be rejected by another compiler. Or the lack of diagnosis may
even cause a defect to go undetected.

You see, the FSF guys want everyone to write in GNU C, that is, using the GCC
extensions, so that the code won't compile with those evil non-freeware
compilers that nobody should be using now that there is GCC. :)



