John Hascall wrote:
>    Is the result of:
> 
>           constant-expression comma-operator constant-expression
> 
>    still a constant-expression or not?  If not, why not (and it

No.

>    seems to me this is the only operator with this property?)

Actually that depends on what exactly you mean. "hello"[0] is not a
constant expression either, even though 'h' and "hello" both are (the
first is integral constant expression and the second is pointer constant
expression).  However, except for the comma operator and operators with
possible side effects, a diagnostic is not required and an
implementation may, at its discretion, accept other forms.

It matters in very few places.  One you have discovered, the other is
exemplified by the fact that (void*)(0,0) is not a null pointer
constant.  The latter again rarely matters because an implementation
gets to choose (but has to document) what (void*)(0,0) becomes, except
in the one context exemplified by the following

int main(void) {
  int a[3];
  *(0? (void*) (0,0) : a) = 0;
  return a[0];
}

is invalid and must get a diagnostic, even though the form with (0,0)
replaced by 0 is perfectly valid C.

> 
> John
> PS, FWIW, gcc accepts both of these and produces the 'expected' results

Old versions of gcc, and current versions if you do not ask it to be an
ANSI C compiler (you need the -pedantic switch).
