In article <cbalmnoIpXQ7@sdg.dra.com>,
	sean@sdg.dra.com (Sean Donelan) writes:

> In Digital Unix 4.0E, the version of the netscape browser distributed
> with the release has a bug which causes the browser to hang for a
> long time the first time it loads a web page containing a Java applet.
> I asked Compaq/Digital customer service, but apparently Netscape is not
> included with the base system support contract. So they wouldn't tell
> me if there is a fix available for the hang in the netscape browser.

The reason for the slow startup the first time that a Java applet is
run is that Netscape needs to load the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).  In
previous versions of Netscape, this was done at program start.

In the newer versions of Netscape, a "startup optimization" was
performed that makes the program start up faster at the expense of the
behavior that you report (loading the JVM when it is first needed).

You can get the old behavior of having the JVM load immediately upon
startup by invoking Netscape with the "-java" option.  This will slow
the program startup, of courrse.

So, it depends on which alternative you consider to be the lesser of
two evils.

-- 
Larry Gensch					      larry.gensch@digital.com



