From: netperf@my-dejanews.com
Subject: Re: NFS client performance of Gigabit NIC ?
Date: 1999/04/09
Message-ID: <7ejlqr$e87$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 464246196
References: <7eefsg$1lm$1@riksun.riken.go.jp>
X-Http-Proxy: 1.0 x17.dejanews.com:80 (Squid/1.1.22) for client 156.153.255.250
Organization: Deja News - The Leader in Internet Discussion
X-Article-Creation-Date: Fri Apr 09 01:40:44 1999 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1
X-Http-User-Agent: Mozilla/4.08 [en] (X11; I; HP-UX B.10.20 9000/735)

Long ago and far away, a new networking card was produced that had 10X the
link-rate of a prior card. This wizzy new card demonstrated 3X the performance
of the old card with a netperf TCP_STREAM test.

However, when someone went to use this new card in an NFS server, things were
actually slower than with the old card.

Why?

NFS is often much more latency sensitive than bandwidth sensitive - consider
things like getattrs and the like, which are small, synchronous request/reply
beasties. Or reads, which are large and (often) synchronous transactions
(modulo the readahead in your client).

Further testing of the wizzy new networking card showed that in addition to
having 3X the bandwidth of the old card, it had 3X the latency - more
accurately, it was only 1/3 the performance of the old card on a netperf
TCP_RR test with sygle byte request/response.

So, you might try measuring netperf TCP_RR across your old and new link in
this case. You might find that in addition to having greater throughput, it
has greater latency...

rick jones

