From: Joerg Czeranski <jc@joerch.org>
Subject: 4.0D/4.0E DEC C optimization bug
Date: 1999/04/10
Message-ID: <5tXNlnALx$2QR$1@joerch.org>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 464912483
Organization: Joerch Software
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Hi,

DEC C V5.6-071 on Digital UNIX V4.0 (Rev. 878),
DEC C V5.7-566 (cc.alt) on Digital UNIX V4.0 (Rev. 878), and
DEC C V5.8-009 on Digital UNIX V4.0 (Rev. 1091)
(I didn't check any others, but AFAIR the same source worked with
the Dec Unix 4.0A compiler)
exhibit a problem compiling sigsetjmp with optimization:

If a local non-volatile automatic variable is written before
the sigsetjmp and read in the "true" branch of "if (sigsetjmp(...))"
but not used elsewhere, the compiler might decide sharing
its location with a different local variable between sigsetjmp
and siglongjmp.

The documentation, the C9X draft, and the Single Unix Spec. say
that such a variable may only have an indeterminant value if it's
written after a setjmp - referring to setjmp/longjmp.

Am I mistaken in assuming the same should apply to sigsetjmp/siglongjmp
or is this a bug?

I found this problem when I compiled the Objective Caml compiler, and
isolated it.
I'll post a source listing that demonstrates it.

joerch



