You didn't mention if you where booted in single-user mode but I suppose sow
because the / filesystem is mounted read-only in single user mode by
default.   Most of the commands use /tmp to store temporarily stuff and /tmp
is not mounted yet (if you use a separate filesystem for it) or part of /.
And this is still read-only!
You have to give the command     mount -u /      first, to remount it
read/write.
If you need all filesystems you are better off giving the command
bcheckrc
In this case you also have /usr  (and /var)  mounted, this gives you all
access to all unix commands.
After that you may continue with the �normal� commands.

You mentioned that you want to copy the / filesystem.     Here�s what I
would do:
Single User mode:
#  bcheckrc
#  mkdir  /var/tmp/root_mount
#  mount -t advfs  temp_dmn#temp_root  /var/tmp/root_mount
or if you are using UFS type (as an example partition /dev/rrz1a )
#  mount -t ufs /dev/rz1a /var/tmp/root_mount
#  vdump  -0f  -  /  |   ( cd  /var/tmp/root_mount ;  vrestore  -xf  - )

If you want to boot from that new partition make sure the label is correct
(and it is the a-partition of the other disk!)
You can make it bootable with the following commands: (again the rz1 as an
example)
#  disklabel  -r  rz1  >  /tmp/label
#  disklabel  -R  -r  -t advfs  /dev/rrz1a   /tmp/label  SCSI
or if you are using UFS type
#  disklabel  -R  -r  -t ufs   /dev/rrz1a   /tmp/label  SCSI

Hopefully this brings you fearther�

