X-Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: UBPG
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

best guess ...

Assuming you can ping the jetdirect box try the following...

Create a generic_text print queue connected to /dev/lp0 .

As ROOT edit the /etc/services file and create a socket name
for a port on the JetDirect. eg:

                hp_port1        9100/tcp
                hp_port2        9101/tcp
                hp_port3        9102/tcp

Now that the services file has been updated, restart the inetd
deamon by sending it a SIGHUP. This needs to be done to reload
the updated services files. eg:

                # ps ax | grep inetd
                  446 ??       I        0:00.17 /usr/sbin/inetd
                  966 ttyp2    S  +     0:00.01 grep inetd
                # kill -HUP 446

          446 is the process ID number and will not be the same.

          ( You could also use the command "rcinet restart" )

Edit the previously created print queue and change the lp value to:

                :lp=@jetdirect_host_name/hp_port1:\

If the printer is on port 2 then use hp_port2, etc..

           Change or add ct setting to `:ct=tcp:\'.

If everything went well you should be able to print.

Example of a completed printcap entry:

pr|lp:\
        :ct=tcp:\
        :if=/usr/lbin/pcfof +Cgeneric_text.pcf:\
        :if=/usr/lbin/ln03rof:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lperr_pr:\
        :lp=@jetdirect_host_name/hp_port1:\
        :mx#0:\
        :of=/usr/lbin/pcfof +Cgeneric_text.pcf:\
        :pl#66:\
        :pw#80:\
        :rw:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/print/pr:\
        :xf=/usr/lbin/xf:

Add :sh:\ to suppress the banner page.

