Message-ID: <7e93k1$dng@ds2.acs.ucalgary.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 462721896
References: <7dd1q4$208$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com> <37061FCA.713AF449@hotmail.com>
Organization: The University of Calgary
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Benoit Gerrienne (bgerrienne@hotmail.com) wrote:
: configuration. But we quickly discover that the system we have installed with
: LSM are slower than the one without it. We've noticed this in the case where the
: server is booting. 

You will definitely observe a significant slowdown on system startup if
you neglect to cleanly shut down LSM volumes (or if you configured your
system with one huge LSM root volume, which IMO is a configuration error).

For clean shutdowns on systems with small root partition, the additional 
LSM overhead on system startup is fairly minimal.

: Some operations can take a lot of time, the loading of huge database 
: for instance (300 MB) is slower. Because LSM is software, you cannot 
: avoid this slowdown.

Yes, but you certainly can configure LSM to minimize the overhead as 
well as to offset it by performance gains due to striping. For example, 
if you expect your workload to contain a large fractions of writes, you 
probably want to have a mirrored set of striped plexes, in which case
the increased throughtput of two or more plex volumes doing your write
in parallel is going to more than offset the software overhead of LSM
mirroring. If you'll try to use catenated plexes in such situation your 
preformance is going to be worse than without LSM, which is hardly a
surprize.

Another issue is that you really want to spead your drives over several
SCSI busses if you are using LSM: with new 10,000 RPM drives, just three
of them can already come close to saturating a wide UltraSCSI bus, and
if you have more than four drives per bus you are probably chocking it
with data (and, of course, using LSM mirroring will double the SCSI
traffic for write operations).

If you have a lot of write traffic, and is limited by the number of
SCSI busses you can have in your system - by all means go for a
hardware RAID solution. On the other hand, LSM is very satisfactory
for many read-mostly setups.

: Another disavantage of LSM is his high level of complexity. You cannot 
: avoid to follow trainings about it. 

I beg to disagree. The LSM administration manual supplied by Digital is
a pretty well writted and complete guide to LSM configuration and 
maintenance. It is more than enough to grasp the principles of LSM
operation (which are far from esoteric) and to configure a useful system.

Regards,

/Serge.P



